history of monitoring

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:18.900
since about 2005 at Google.

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:21.765
We had a binary
there called Borgman.

00:02:21.765 --> 00:02:23.140
It's actually--
it's in the book,

00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:26.210
but you can think of it as
Prometheus in recent terms.

00:02:26.210 --> 00:02:29.270
Prometheus is sort of an
external recreation of Borgmon.

00:02:29.270 --> 00:02:32.930
It's a single binary
collection evaluation

00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:34.560
type monitoring system.

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:38.720
And every team would run
their own, the core binary,

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:41.770
and it has service scale, but
you have to scale it manually.

00:02:41.770 --> 00:02:45.580
You had to arrange it in tiers
and like, pass up aggregations

00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:47.494
towards the global tier.

00:02:47.494 --> 00:02:49.160
And there was already
sort of a movement

00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:51.560
towards centralization,
so you'd have some support

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:53.960
infrastructure that
initially was per team,

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:57.680
but migrated to be run by
what then became production

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:02.427
monitoring SRE in about 2007
or so that I'm now a part of.

00:03:02.427 --> 00:03:04.760
This infrastructure included
things like metamonitoring.

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:07.560
So you want to monitor that to
your Borgmons actually work,

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:09.399
otherwise monitoring
is kind of useless.

00:03:09.399 --> 00:03:10.940
You'd have long term
history storage,

00:03:10.940 --> 00:03:13.310
so you could serve queries
over multiple years,

00:03:13.310 --> 00:03:16.550
and you would have the
alerting outbound part.

00:03:16.550 --> 00:03:19.150
And in 2012, [INAUDIBLE]
took on Monarch

00:03:19.150 --> 00:03:20.510
as the new state of the art.

00:03:20.510 --> 00:03:23.480
Monarch is a large,
distributed monitoring system.

00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:26.060
If you're interested,
there's a great talk by one

00:03:26.060 --> 00:03:27.590
of the Monarch
TLs, John Banning,

00:03:27.590 --> 00:03:31.940
given at Monitorama 2016,
you can find it online.

00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:34.310
It is Google Scale
in one system,

00:03:34.310 --> 00:03:37.160
so we actually literally monitor
all of Google in one monitoring

00:03:37.160 --> 00:03:41.300
system, and it is monitoring
as a service in that as a user,

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:42.800
you only have to
provide a conflict.

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:44.610
This still requires
