been using for years at Google.

00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:07.850
So instead of that, it
runs on top of Monarch

00:04:07.850 --> 00:04:10.340
and on top of our alert
management system,

00:04:10.340 --> 00:04:12.680
and it uses the
years of experience

00:04:12.680 --> 00:04:14.740
of running services at scale.

00:04:14.740 --> 00:04:19.730
But Stackdriver is geared solely
towards users of the cloud.

00:04:19.730 --> 00:04:23.570
It was built for monitoring
cloud applications,

00:04:23.570 --> 00:04:28.520
with a focus on easy getting
started experience, very

00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:33.750
user friendly UI, and Mantra,
which is still true today,

00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:35.750
which we strive to--

00:04:35.750 --> 00:04:39.770
you don't have to be
an expert in monitoring

00:04:39.770 --> 00:04:42.150
to monitor your services well.

00:04:42.150 --> 00:04:45.530
So we prefer that you
focus your time and energy

00:04:45.530 --> 00:04:49.220
more on innovation and
less on administration.

00:04:49.220 --> 00:04:52.010
So that was true when
we acquired Stackdriver,

00:04:52.010 --> 00:04:54.590
and it's true today.

00:04:54.590 --> 00:04:57.770
Stackdriver has evolved to
be more than just monitoring.

00:04:57.770 --> 00:05:01.220
So it offers a wide
range of other services,

00:05:01.220 --> 00:05:05.090
and it supports not
just GCP but also AWS.

00:05:05.090 --> 00:05:09.935
I'll cover that more
towards the end of our talk.

00:05:09.935 --> 00:05:13.830
So now let's go over some
definitions, and so--

00:05:13.830 --> 00:05:16.980
we're sure that we're all kind
of speaking the same language.

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:21.480
So these are commonly used terms
in monitoring and alerting.

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:24.810
Sometimes they're overloaded,
especially the alerting part.

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:27.270
So when we talk
about monitoring,

00:05:27.270 --> 00:05:30.870
we're talking about you
know, measuring performance,

00:05:30.870 --> 00:05:34.830
correctness, observing--

00:05:34.830 --> 00:05:37.260
building instrumentation,
so that's really

00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:41.790
extraction of metrics from our
application, from our services

00:05:41.790 --> 00:05:44.760
into something useful that
we can use for dashboarding

00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:46.170
and for alerting.

00:05:46.170 --> 00:05:49.530
Logging is another type
of information extraction.

00:05:49.530 --> 00:05:53.310
Logs are a little different than
regular metrics, in the sense

00:05:53.310 --> 00:05:55.660
that they are immutable.

00:05:55.660 --> 00:05:59.670
They have a timestamp, but they
can also serve for monitoring.

00:05:59.670 --> 00:06:02.160
For example,
extracting metrics from

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:04.870
logs, putting them in
dashboards, alerting on them,

00:06:04.870 --> 00:06:06.180
et cetera, et cetera.

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:09.570
Alerting, again, probably
the most overloaded term,

00:06:09.570 --> 00:06:12.060
and it can be a verb and a noun.

00:06:12.060 --> 00:06:15.030
So alerting is the process
of also configuring

00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:18.570
your alert rules, and, you
know, maintaining them.

00:06:18.570 --> 00:06:22.230
It's also the process of
detecting issues, opening

00:06:22.230 --> 00:06:26.490
incidents, and informing is
sometimes also called alerting.

00:06:26.490 --> 00:06:30.960
So the act of informing
users that something

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:33.790
is wrong with the services
that you're running.

00:06:33.790 --> 00:06:37.440
Then we have debugging, or
figuring out the root cause.

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:39.660
That happens usually
after alerting.

00:06:39.660 --> 00:06:41.310
It's getting down
to the problem,

00:06:41.310 --> 00:06:44.340
it's mitigating the
problems, and at the end

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:47.910
when all is well
again, we sometimes

00:06:47.910 --> 00:06:50.520
do postmortems,
kind of to make sure

00:06:50.520 --> 00:06:53.670
that whatever happened
does not happen again

00:06:53.670 --> 00:06:57.235
or is treated more efficiently.

00:06:57.235 --> 00:06:59.360
THOMAS RAST: User impact
will be an important term,

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:03.220
and we gave it its own slide for
three definitions, the SL A, I,

00:07:03.220 --> 00:07:07.970
and O. To measure
something like user impact,

00:07:07.970 --> 00:07:11.310
SREs especially sets themselves
service level objectives.

00:07:11.310 --> 00:07:13.657
So as an example, you might
have an uptime objective,

00:07:13.657 --> 00:07:15.740
we're using uptime here
as sort of a health check.

00:07:15.740 --> 00:07:18.980
You'd just issue an HTTP request
and see if it comes back,

00:07:18.980 --> 00:07:20.210
of say, three 9s, right.

00:07:20.210 --> 00:07:22.310
99.9% success.

00:07:22.310 --> 00:07:24.512
And for every SLO,
you would track

00:07:24.512 --> 00:07:25.970
the corresponding
indicator, that's

00:07:25.970 --> 00:07:29.199
your SLI, which in
this case is uptime.

00:07:29.199 --> 00:07:31.490
There's another term, SLA,
which is the actual business

00:07:31.490 --> 00:07:33.350
agreement entered
into with customers.

00:07:33.350 --> 00:07:35.077
The distinction
between A and O is

00:07:35.077 --> 00:07:37.160
kind of irrelevant at this
talk, so we'll skip it.

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:45.270
One note here is
if you were aiming

00:07:45.270 --> 00:07:47.400
to go for like, five
nines, because you heard,

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:49.920
I don't know, Google
does it or something,

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:52.470
be aware, with like
everything you hear later,

00:07:52.470 --> 00:07:54.840
you only have five minutes
per year for your entire page

00:07:54.840 --> 00:07:57.720
response up to mitigation if you
actually have anything happen.

00:08:00.270 --> 00:08:01.020
AMIR HERMELIN: OK.

00:08:01.020 --> 00:08:04.490
So before we dive into the
monitoring philosophy and best

00:08:04.490 --> 00:08:07.040
practices, et cetera,
it's very important

00:08:07.040 --> 00:08:10.940
to look at the process around
alerting and around monitoring

00:08:10.940 --> 00:08:12.770
at a higher level.

00:08:12.770 --> 00:08:15.240
So Thomas mentioned SLOs.

00:08:15.240 --> 00:08:17.897
We start by actually
defining them.

00:08:17.897 --> 00:08:19.730
You have to realize
what you want to monitor

00:08:19.730 --> 00:08:21.020
before you start monitoring.

00:08:21.020 --> 00:08:25.430
It sounds trivial, but we
find that sometimes people

00:08:25.430 --> 00:08:27.230
do it the other way.

00:08:27.230 --> 00:08:30.237
So think about what are your
SLOs, what you want to monitor,

00:08:30.237 --> 00:08:31.820
what do you want to
put on dashboards,

00:08:31.820 --> 00:08:33.409
what are you on instrument.

00:08:33.409 --> 00:08:35.120
You make sure you
get those metrics,

00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:36.935
either from the system
that you're using,

00:08:36.935 --> 00:08:39.860
via system level metrics
or custom metrics,

00:08:39.860 --> 00:08:41.419
and then when you
have all that, you

00:08:41.419 --> 00:08:43.970
can start actually
configuring your alerts.

00:08:43.970 --> 00:08:47.690
So you know what you're alerting
on, everything all is well,

00:08:47.690 --> 00:08:49.790
and then an alert triggers.

00:08:49.790 --> 00:08:52.190
So one thing we want to
emphasize with this diagram

00:08:52.190 --> 00:08:54.651
is this process is iterative.

00:08:54.651 --> 00:08:56.150
You're never really
at a point where

00:08:56.150 --> 00:08:58.340
you have the perfect
configuration,

00:08:58.340 --> 00:08:59.810
and there's nothing more to do.

00:08:59.810 --> 00:09:02.330
Because things
change, realizations

00:09:02.330 --> 00:09:05.570
change, SLOs change,
your service changes.

00:09:05.570 --> 00:09:07.170
So it's an iterative process.

00:09:07.170 --> 00:09:09.070
But especially in
the beginning, you

00:09:09.070 --> 00:09:11.681
should be prepared
to kind of change

00:09:11.681 --> 00:09:13.430
your alerting rules
and the way you alert.

00:09:13.430 --> 00:09:18.080
So if an alert triggers,
usually an incident is open,

00:09:18.080 --> 00:09:21.590
we use that term to
define like a problem

00:09:21.590 --> 00:09:23.120
that somebody needs
to take care of.

00:09:23.120 --> 00:09:26.100
So there a time span
an incident is active,

00:09:26.100 --> 00:09:27.750
and notification is being sent.

00:09:27.750 --> 00:09:31.220
So somebody is
handling the issue.

00:09:31.220 --> 00:09:34.160
Now, the first thing to figure
out is if this issue is real.

00:09:34.160 --> 00:09:38.690
If it is real, you need to
mitigate it, you know, debug,

00:09:38.690 --> 00:09:39.890
maybe escalate.

00:09:39.890 --> 00:09:42.464
But if it's not real, you need
to look at the alerting policy

00:09:42.464 --> 00:09:44.630
that triggered rather than
just going-- getting back

00:09:44.630 --> 00:09:48.560
to work and not waste time the
next time it triggers, and see

00:09:48.560 --> 00:09:51.200
what you need to maybe
change in the alert policy

00:09:51.200 --> 00:09:53.060
or maybe delete.

00:09:53.060 --> 00:09:55.880
When all is said and done,
the incident is resolved.

00:09:55.880 --> 00:09:58.940
If it was real and if
it was serious enough,

00:09:58.940 --> 00:10:01.640
maybe a post-mortem is
needed, and we go back

00:10:01.640 --> 00:10:05.260
to this cycle of iterating
on your alerting policies.

00:10:07.800 --> 00:10:10.711
So now let's segue--

00:10:10.711 --> 00:10:12.460
you know, we're done
with the definitions,

00:10:12.460 --> 00:10:15.760
and we want to segue into a
little bit about monitoring,

00:10:15.760 --> 00:10:18.550
what it is, and what
drives good monitoring.

00:10:18.550 --> 00:10:22.630
So why do we monitor, right?

00:10:22.630 --> 00:10:25.960
Why are you here, why do you
use a monitoring solution?

00:10:25.960 --> 00:10:28.840
So these are kind of the--

00:10:28.840 --> 00:10:31.840
probably the main use cases.

00:10:31.840 --> 00:10:37.900
We monitor because we want
to detect and analyze trends.

00:10:37.900 --> 00:10:41.770
It could be business metrics,
it could be service metrics.

00:10:41.770 --> 00:10:45.250
We want to see for latency is
trending up or trending down,

00:10:45.250 --> 00:10:47.710
or some resources are
trending to depletion.

00:10:47.710 --> 00:10:50.500
I want to compare over time,
for example, its business

00:10:50.500 --> 00:10:52.570
metrics we want know if
[INAUDIBLE] checkouts,

00:10:52.570 --> 00:10:56.690
how do they compare it to the
last season or the last month?

00:10:56.690 --> 00:10:58.210
If it's latency,
how does latency

00:10:58.210 --> 00:11:01.840
compare to our error ratio
compared to last week?

00:11:01.840 --> 00:11:04.360
We build dashboards, so we use
metrics to build dashboards.

00:11:04.360 --> 00:11:06.940
So we have an at a
glance introspection

00:11:06.940 --> 00:11:10.300
into our services, rather
than having to go and do

00:11:10.300 --> 00:11:12.490
a lot of work each time we
want to get some answers

00:11:12.490 --> 00:11:14.440
to basic questions.

00:11:14.440 --> 00:11:17.770
Of course, we use
metrics for alerting,

00:11:17.770 --> 00:11:21.610
which is the main topic
of this discussion,

00:11:21.610 --> 00:11:25.160
and we also-- debugging
is not part of monitoring,

00:11:25.160 --> 00:11:27.370
but it's very
strongly tied into it.

00:11:27.370 --> 00:11:29.770
We use, sometimes,
instrument metrics

00:11:29.770 --> 00:11:32.170
that will help us debug,
that will help us drill down.

00:11:32.170 --> 00:11:34.300
We will go into that later.

00:11:34.300 --> 00:11:36.400
Sometimes we instrument
dashboards just

00:11:36.400 --> 00:11:40.660
for the debug part, just
for the firefighting part.

00:11:40.660 --> 00:11:44.870
So it's important
to mention that.

00:11:44.870 --> 00:11:47.380
So what you really want to
make sure you're monitoring.

00:11:47.380 --> 00:11:50.200
This is what we call
the four golden signals.

00:11:50.200 --> 00:11:54.790
And that's a painting
of the four horsemen,

00:11:54.790 --> 00:11:57.760
so if you want to make
sure your service doesn't

00:11:57.760 --> 00:12:00.730
get to Armageddon
level, you want

00:12:00.730 --> 00:12:03.610
to make sure that you're
monitoring these things.

00:12:03.610 --> 00:12:04.330
At least, right?

00:12:04.330 --> 00:12:08.152
The first two are errors,
or error ratio, and latency.

00:12:08.152 --> 00:12:09.610
Why this is important,
this is kind

00:12:09.610 --> 00:12:12.190
of-- these are kind
of the outbound facing

00:12:12.190 --> 00:12:14.110
metrics of your service.

00:12:14.110 --> 00:12:16.870
This is how your users
view your service.

00:12:16.870 --> 00:12:19.720
So if your latency is bad, or
if you have too many errors,

00:12:19.720 --> 00:12:22.300
that's immediately reflected
in the quality of service

00:12:22.300 --> 00:12:23.680
that you provide.

00:12:23.680 --> 00:12:26.680
The other two, traffic,
which is self-explanatory,

00:12:26.680 --> 00:12:31.000
and saturation, which is really
the level that your resources

00:12:31.000 --> 00:12:33.070
are full or the level
that you're utilizing

00:12:33.070 --> 00:12:34.940
your resources, you
can look at these

00:12:34.940 --> 00:12:38.890
as king of the view of your
service towards the inside.

00:12:38.890 --> 00:12:41.260
So this is what your
service, if it was a person,

00:12:41.260 --> 00:12:42.655
this is what it would be seeing.

00:12:42.655 --> 00:12:45.220
It's seeing the traffic,
and seeing the saturation

00:12:45.220 --> 00:12:46.210
internally.

00:12:46.210 --> 00:12:48.770
And these two can
lead to error--

00:12:48.770 --> 00:12:50.710
you know, to errors
or bad experiences,

00:12:50.710 --> 00:12:54.820
that will eventually
reflect on your users.

00:12:54.820 --> 00:12:57.010
THOMAS RAST: So you're
measuring these four signals,

00:12:57.010 --> 00:12:58.540
then there's a
question of how, right.

00:12:58.540 --> 00:13:00.289
And we have an important
distinction here,

00:13:00.289 --> 00:13:03.040
which is that the difference
between black box, or probing,

00:13:03.040 --> 00:13:04.740
and white box monitoring.

00:13:04.740 --> 00:13:08.230
So black box is if you
monitor from the outside,

00:13:08.230 --> 00:13:11.177
treating the system as a
black box, hence the name.

00:13:11.177 --> 00:13:12.760
The advantage here
is it's independent

00:13:12.760 --> 00:13:14.260
in terms of failure
modes, at least

00:13:14.260 --> 00:13:17.110
largely, usually, which
makes it more reliable

00:13:17.110 --> 00:13:19.194
and is better for alerting.

00:13:19.194 --> 00:13:20.860
One example here, I
mean, you might have

00:13:20.860 --> 00:13:23.052
[INAUDIBLE] time check again.

00:13:23.052 --> 00:13:25.510
For a stateful system you may
want a stateful probe, right?

00:13:25.510 --> 00:13:27.777
You may want to, I don't
know, you have a file system,

00:13:27.777 --> 00:13:29.860
you write a new file, put
some content in, read it

00:13:29.860 --> 00:13:31.651
back, see if it comes
back, and then delete

00:13:31.651 --> 00:13:32.780
it again to clean up.

00:13:32.780 --> 00:13:35.950
And the opposite is monitoring
that works and the cooperation

00:13:35.950 --> 00:13:37.270
of the system, which is great.

00:13:37.270 --> 00:13:39.209
Because you get a
lot more detail.

00:13:39.209 --> 00:13:41.500
And you'll need that detail
to do the actual debugging.

00:13:41.500 --> 00:13:43.720
But again, the failure
modes may be tight,

00:13:43.720 --> 00:13:47.367
and so you usually don't want
to rely on this for alerting.

00:13:47.367 --> 00:13:49.450
A good example of one thing
you could not possibly

00:13:49.450 --> 00:13:52.030
get from black box is
the bottom example.

00:13:52.030 --> 00:13:53.770
If you compared
the actual memory

00:13:53.770 --> 00:13:56.954
handed out by your
malloc, versus the memory

00:13:56.954 --> 00:13:58.870
given to your process
by the operating system.

00:13:58.870 --> 00:14:01.360
There's no way you can get
that without it being exported

00:14:01.360 --> 00:14:03.790
from your process, right.

00:14:03.790 --> 00:14:06.790
AMIR HERMELIN: So just
to put this in context

00:14:06.790 --> 00:14:09.640
with the monitoring
solution that we offer,

00:14:09.640 --> 00:14:11.440
just a show of hands,
how many of you

00:14:11.440 --> 00:14:15.120
are familiar with Stackdriver?

00:14:15.120 --> 00:14:16.870
Great, let's see--

00:14:16.870 --> 00:14:19.480
83%.

00:14:19.480 --> 00:14:19.980
That's good.

00:14:19.980 --> 00:14:23.235
The ones that aren't,
you should be, but.

00:14:23.235 --> 00:14:24.915
The way we offer
these, for example,

00:14:24.915 --> 00:14:28.560
in Stackdriver, which most of
you should be familiar with

00:14:28.560 --> 00:14:30.570
is--

00:14:30.570 --> 00:14:33.140
black box monitoring
is our uptime check,

00:14:33.140 --> 00:14:36.450
and you should be using that
to set up uptime checks.

00:14:36.450 --> 00:14:38.040
To set up alerts
on these kind of--

00:14:38.040 --> 00:14:40.590
and use probes from the outside.

00:14:40.590 --> 00:14:43.290
White box monitoring,
so for example,

00:14:43.290 --> 00:14:45.750
the system level metrics
that are provided

00:14:45.750 --> 00:14:49.620
for your resources, for
example custom metrics that you

00:14:49.620 --> 00:14:50.850
as users need to instrument.

00:14:50.850 --> 00:14:54.680
For example, the logs that are
available via the Stackdriver

00:14:54.680 --> 00:14:55.500
suites.

00:14:55.500 --> 00:14:58.482
All of that is white
box monitoring.

00:14:58.482 --> 00:15:00.440
THOMAS RAST: We have two
slides here about sort

00:15:00.440 --> 00:15:02.000
of a pet peeve of mine.

00:15:02.000 --> 00:15:05.259
An FAQ with monitoring
is, what is the difference

00:15:05.259 --> 00:15:06.300
between those two graphs?

00:15:06.300 --> 00:15:09.560
They're traffic,
monitored at the system.

00:15:09.560 --> 00:15:12.181
In this case, the
difference is sampling rate.

00:15:12.181 --> 00:15:14.180
So I made artificial
graphs to show the example,

00:15:14.180 --> 00:15:16.310
but the left one has one
minute sampling rates

00:15:16.310 --> 00:15:19.050
and the right one has
one second sampling rate.

00:15:19.050 --> 00:15:21.870
Clicky.

00:15:21.870 --> 00:15:24.450
If you have very short
spikes on the minute,

00:15:24.450 --> 00:15:27.720
for example, which happens
surprisingly often,

00:15:27.720 --> 00:15:30.510
you will never see them on
a typical one minute sampled

00:15:30.510 --> 00:15:31.780
monitoring system.

00:15:31.780 --> 00:15:33.750
And again, one minute
is really typical.

00:15:33.750 --> 00:15:35.460
One second has a
significant overhead

00:15:35.460 --> 00:15:39.030
in terms of how often you need
to go talk to your system,

00:15:39.030 --> 00:15:41.870
how much it takes
to store that data.

00:15:41.870 --> 00:15:44.720
And another one is, if
you look at distributions,

00:15:44.720 --> 00:15:47.090
latencies in particular,
so the horizontal axis here

00:15:47.090 --> 00:15:48.332
is latency.

00:15:48.332 --> 00:15:49.790
If you're just
looking at the mean,

00:15:49.790 --> 00:15:52.512
and maybe, I don't
know, mean plus sigmas,

00:15:52.512 --> 00:15:53.720
you're already missing a lot.

00:15:53.720 --> 00:15:55.136
Maybe you heard
about percentiles.

00:15:55.136 --> 00:15:58.190
Percentiles are important,
but then the distribution

00:15:58.190 --> 00:16:01.955
that motivated this slide
looks something like this.

00:16:01.955 --> 00:16:06.920
This is from a caching
front-end for a storage system,

00:16:06.920 --> 00:16:10.160
and so the behavior you see
is the very low latencies,

00:16:10.160 --> 00:16:13.070
like sub-10 milliseconds
is a cache hit.

00:16:13.070 --> 00:16:16.070
And the bump at something
like 600 milliseconds

00:16:16.070 --> 00:16:18.315
is if it has to go
talk to its back end.

00:16:18.315 --> 00:16:19.940
And this is exceedingly
common, and you

00:16:19.940 --> 00:16:22.550
will fail to see it if you
only look at latencies--

00:16:22.550 --> 00:16:25.740
percentiles, excuse me.

00:16:25.740 --> 00:16:28.890
And this now gets
us to the philosophy

00:16:28.890 --> 00:16:30.960
that you've been waiting for.

00:16:30.960 --> 00:16:32.754
And we have three core points.

00:16:32.754 --> 00:16:34.170
One, you should
really be alerting

00:16:34.170 --> 00:16:37.000
on symptoms using SLOs.

00:16:37.000 --> 00:16:38.430
We will have
something that you'll

00:16:38.430 --> 00:16:40.888
see in a slide in a moment that
we call the JUAN criterion.

00:16:40.888 --> 00:16:42.780
It's really good to remember it.

00:16:42.780 --> 00:16:46.450
And we have a human
aspect that we'll discuss.

00:16:46.450 --> 00:16:48.990
AMIR HERMELIN: So
the first thing--

00:16:48.990 --> 00:16:51.000
this is a section, by the
way, about philosophy,

00:16:51.000 --> 00:16:52.680
so we're going to
talk about the why,

00:16:52.680 --> 00:16:55.920
and later we're going to show
some best practices more on the

00:16:55.920 --> 00:16:57.210
how.

00:16:57.210 --> 00:17:02.650
But the philosophy around
alerting on symptoms.

00:17:02.650 --> 00:17:06.420
This focuses-- you
ask somebody that's

00:17:06.420 --> 00:17:09.359
monitoring their services
on the things that

00:17:09.359 --> 00:17:11.460
really matter to your users.

00:17:11.460 --> 00:17:15.780
So by alerting only on
symptoms, or the vast majority

00:17:15.780 --> 00:17:19.800
of your alerts related to
symptoms, that makes sure A,

00:17:19.800 --> 00:17:22.619
that you're not wasting
time on meaningless alerts.

00:17:22.619 --> 00:17:24.469
That may be alerting
on things that you

00:17:24.469 --> 00:17:27.780
know you could avoid
notifying in the first place,

00:17:27.780 --> 00:17:31.380
and also it focuses you on
really being-- the alerts being

00:17:31.380 --> 00:17:36.900
tied to the quality of service
that your users are seeing.

00:17:36.900 --> 00:17:40.110
The second part of our
philosophy is JUAN metrics.

00:17:40.110 --> 00:17:42.180
These are metrics
that you should

00:17:42.180 --> 00:17:45.240
put every alerting
policy through

00:17:45.240 --> 00:17:48.150
to make sure that it's valid.

00:17:48.150 --> 00:17:51.030
Assuming you have a
finite budget of time,

00:17:51.030 --> 00:17:54.450
you don't want to be wasting
time on meaningless alerts.

00:17:54.450 --> 00:17:57.900
So any alert that
doesn't pass this test

00:17:57.900 --> 00:18:01.020
should be a candidate
for removal.

00:18:01.020 --> 00:18:03.080
The first one is Judgment.

00:18:03.080 --> 00:18:05.340
An alert should
require human judgment.

00:18:05.340 --> 00:18:08.790
Otherwise, why waste
a person's time?

00:18:08.790 --> 00:18:12.180
And also, it can really upset
people when they get frequently

00:18:12.180 --> 00:18:14.640
alerted and they
feel like you know,

00:18:14.640 --> 00:18:17.370
they don't have to
think or do anything,

00:18:17.370 --> 00:18:21.170
it's just-- they don't
have to use any judgment.

00:18:21.170 --> 00:18:23.310
And that causes churn.

00:18:23.310 --> 00:18:26.760
It should be Urgent, either
right now or next business

00:18:26.760 --> 00:18:29.940
day, because again, if
something isn't urgent,

00:18:29.940 --> 00:18:33.060
why are you diverting focus
from other more important things

00:18:33.060 --> 00:18:36.780
to this if it can wait for
a week or even a month?

00:18:36.780 --> 00:18:39.150
It should be Actionable.

00:18:39.150 --> 00:18:43.140
This one should be clear,
but I want to explain it.

00:18:43.140 --> 00:18:45.720
It means that there
should be some action that

00:18:45.720 --> 00:18:48.600
needs to be taken as a
result of this alert,

00:18:48.600 --> 00:18:52.000
other than reading the
alert notification.

00:18:52.000 --> 00:18:53.620
Now it doesn't mean--

00:18:53.620 --> 00:18:59.340
it doesn't mean that you
can mitigate the problem.

00:18:59.340 --> 00:19:02.990
Take, for example, I mean,
we know cloud providers,

00:19:02.990 --> 00:19:04.920
they're always failure free.

00:19:04.920 --> 00:19:06.990
But let's say your
cloud provider

00:19:06.990 --> 00:19:08.436
has some sort of failure.

00:19:08.436 --> 00:19:10.560
You should still know about
it, even though there's

00:19:10.560 --> 00:19:13.530
not much you can do, except
email me and others and "Hey,

00:19:13.530 --> 00:19:14.370
what's up?"

00:19:14.370 --> 00:19:16.980
But even then you
want to maybe explain

00:19:16.980 --> 00:19:20.040
to your users what's going
on, or through your page,

00:19:20.040 --> 00:19:21.930
or through your
Twitter page, you know.

00:19:21.930 --> 00:19:26.040
React to that, be on high
alert to maybe later divert

00:19:26.040 --> 00:19:27.445
traffic et cetera, et cetera.

00:19:27.445 --> 00:19:29.570
So that's an example where
something is actionable,

00:19:29.570 --> 00:19:34.090
but it's not something that you
can do with fixing the service.

00:19:34.090 --> 00:19:38.550
And last but not least,
definitely, is Necessary.

00:19:38.550 --> 00:19:43.290
If it doesn't affect anything,
users, service, business,

00:19:43.290 --> 00:19:44.220
why alert on that?

00:19:44.220 --> 00:19:48.470
Maybe there are other,
more efficient ways.

00:19:48.470 --> 00:19:50.030
THOMAS RAST: The human aspects.

00:19:50.030 --> 00:19:51.620
The most important
one, we think,

00:19:51.620 --> 00:19:54.650
is you have to consider
the stress budget.

00:19:54.650 --> 00:19:56.740
Every alert triggers
a stress response.

00:19:56.740 --> 00:19:58.904
This is good, right, you
need this adrenaline boost

00:19:58.904 --> 00:20:00.320
to deal with the
alert, especially

00:20:00.320 --> 00:20:03.140
if you're getting in the middle
of the night or something.

00:20:03.140 --> 00:20:05.080
But this has a
cost to the human.

00:20:05.080 --> 00:20:07.610
It's a very biological
thing, and you

00:20:07.610 --> 00:20:09.530
need to make that budget last.

00:20:09.530 --> 00:20:12.020
And there are two basic
ways you can do this.

00:20:12.020 --> 00:20:13.910
You can keep the frequency
of alerting down,

00:20:13.910 --> 00:20:18.320
just the pure number, or you can
make the human more confident

00:20:18.320 --> 00:20:20.750
and thereby reduce the stress
for the individual alert.

00:20:20.750 --> 00:20:24.080
And we call this providing help,
because if you support them

00:20:24.080 --> 00:20:26.880
in especially the very
beginning of the response,

00:20:26.880 --> 00:20:29.720
if they're better trained,
they have better documentation,

00:20:29.720 --> 00:20:31.830
they will experience
less stress.

00:20:31.830 --> 00:20:33.701
And the second
aspect of humans is

00:20:33.701 --> 00:20:35.950
they're good at pattern
recognition and somewhat lazy.

00:20:35.950 --> 00:20:37.370
Again, that's
[INAUDIBLE] automate

00:20:37.370 --> 00:20:39.330
themselves out of their jobs.

00:20:39.330 --> 00:20:42.290
In alerts especially, this
means that specifically

00:20:42.290 --> 00:20:44.614
for actionable,
mostly, people will

00:20:44.614 --> 00:20:46.280
realize that something
is not actionable

00:20:46.280 --> 00:20:48.965
and start ignoring it,
at least subconsciously.

00:20:51.940 --> 00:20:53.650
And so we have the
three core points.

00:20:53.650 --> 00:20:58.730
Again, symptom based SLO alerts,
JUAN, and the human aspect,

00:20:58.730 --> 00:21:01.224
and we'll now try and develop
some actual best practices

00:21:01.224 --> 00:21:02.140
that follow from this.

00:21:04.660 --> 00:21:07.312
First for SLOs, how do
you choose then an SLO?

00:21:07.312 --> 00:21:09.520
All right, this is not
necessarily an alerting thing,

00:21:09.520 --> 00:21:12.700
but it's very important because
it's the start of alerting.

00:21:12.700 --> 00:21:14.530
You should really
bound it between what

00:21:14.530 --> 00:21:16.900
your service can support
and what your customers can

00:21:16.900 --> 00:21:17.470
support, all right.

00:21:17.470 --> 00:21:19.094
So the service gives
you an upper bound

00:21:19.094 --> 00:21:23.560
of how much you can ask, and
if you get that wrong, you've

00:21:23.560 --> 00:21:26.110
put the SLO too tight, your
oncallers will burn out.

00:21:26.110 --> 00:21:28.568
That's like the number one
risk, is you get too many pages,

00:21:28.568 --> 00:21:30.920
your people burn out.

00:21:30.920 --> 00:21:33.190
If you get the
lower bound wrong,

00:21:33.190 --> 00:21:34.690
then your customers will leave.

00:21:34.690 --> 00:21:36.630
So you have to get
it between those.

00:21:36.630 --> 00:21:40.180
And just as a side note, the
three nines of uptime as an SRE

00:21:40.180 --> 00:21:42.730
tenant, also means you
have 0.1% of downtime

00:21:42.730 --> 00:21:48.020
you can use to break stuff,
take risks, do something.

00:21:48.020 --> 00:21:51.260
So So we want to augment that--

00:21:51.260 --> 00:21:53.270
are you all familiar
with the fail whale

00:21:53.270 --> 00:21:55.205
and Twitter early days?

00:21:55.205 --> 00:21:57.080
And we actually want to
bring up this example

00:21:57.080 --> 00:21:58.410
as a positive example.

00:21:58.410 --> 00:22:00.170
You can't always
be a perfectionist,

00:22:00.170 --> 00:22:04.200
and you can't always meet
SLOs that please your users.

00:22:04.200 --> 00:22:07.640
And sometimes you have to
make decisions and business

00:22:07.640 --> 00:22:11.480
decisions, such as not meeting
your SLOs because you're

00:22:11.480 --> 00:22:13.500
experiencing huge growth.

00:22:13.500 --> 00:22:15.574
And the size of the
team, and the size

00:22:15.574 --> 00:22:17.240
of the new users
you're getting everyday

00:22:17.240 --> 00:22:19.814
is just too much
to meet your SLOs.

00:22:19.814 --> 00:22:22.230
And that's fine, as long as
you make a conscious decision,

00:22:22.230 --> 00:22:24.050
say you know what,
a lot of my users

00:22:24.050 --> 00:22:26.480
are going to be seeing
this, or high latency,

00:22:26.480 --> 00:22:29.360
or not being able to log
in, because right now it's

00:22:29.360 --> 00:22:32.000
all hands on deck
focusing on growth.

00:22:32.000 --> 00:22:34.610
But it has to be, of course,
in a controlled manner,

00:22:34.610 --> 00:22:39.015
and you have to know why
you're not meeting your SLOs.

00:22:41.580 --> 00:22:47.420
So let's look at
one's example metrics.

00:22:47.420 --> 00:22:50.820
So we talk-- we
talked about why,

00:22:50.820 --> 00:22:54.756
but let's take a few
examples for each--

00:22:54.756 --> 00:22:58.320
for each of the
kind of four tests.

00:22:58.320 --> 00:23:00.600
So first of all, judgment.

00:23:00.600 --> 00:23:03.870
Good judgment is when
there's SLO alert,

00:23:03.870 --> 00:23:06.769
right, you have to determine
where the problem is,

00:23:06.769 --> 00:23:08.310
or there's some roll
out and you have

00:23:08.310 --> 00:23:10.830
to determine if the
release is good.

00:23:10.830 --> 00:23:13.170
Or maybe you have to
roll back the release.

00:23:13.170 --> 00:23:15.540
So you have to make
some judgment there.

00:23:15.540 --> 00:23:19.410
Bad example is when automated
actions, such as server

00:23:19.410 --> 00:23:21.510
restarts, take care of
things and you still

00:23:21.510 --> 00:23:22.410
get alerted on it.

00:23:22.410 --> 00:23:25.020
Even though, you know,
the automated action

00:23:25.020 --> 00:23:27.750
took care of things,
or when something can--

00:23:27.750 --> 00:23:30.690
it isn't, but it can-- be
simply fixed with autoscaling.

00:23:30.690 --> 00:23:33.030
If autoscaling is
doing its job, you

00:23:33.030 --> 00:23:35.730
shouldn't be alerted on
it, even if resources

00:23:35.730 --> 00:23:39.060
are kind of being stressed.

00:23:39.060 --> 00:23:41.200
Another test is urgent.

00:23:41.200 --> 00:23:44.910
So definitely SLO
and security issues

00:23:44.910 --> 00:23:46.200
both are very, very urgent.

00:23:46.200 --> 00:23:48.220
You should alert on all of them.

00:23:48.220 --> 00:23:50.500
But if you find yourself
saying something like,

00:23:50.500 --> 00:23:53.880
well, you know, if
this doesn't get fixed,

00:23:53.880 --> 00:23:57.060
doesn't resolve itself in 12
hours, then we'll look at it.

00:23:57.060 --> 00:24:00.120
That's a candidate to
kind of revisit the alert.

00:24:00.120 --> 00:24:04.150
Maybe, again, you're
wasting time there.

00:24:04.150 --> 00:24:06.400
And it's important-- the
reason we're emphasizing this

00:24:06.400 --> 00:24:11.040
is, again, you have a limited
budget for your oncaller turn.

00:24:11.040 --> 00:24:13.140
So you want to
spend that budget,

00:24:13.140 --> 00:24:14.700
not get to alert
fatigue, you want

00:24:14.700 --> 00:24:17.460
to spend it on the right things
versus the wrong things that

00:24:17.460 --> 00:24:19.320
don't pass this test.

00:24:19.320 --> 00:24:21.210
So another thing is actionable.

00:24:21.210 --> 00:24:23.940
When you need to determine if
something needs rollback, when

00:24:23.940 --> 00:24:27.240
you need to determine if
you need to add capacity

00:24:27.240 --> 00:24:30.650
and it's not
automatically added.

00:24:30.650 --> 00:24:34.380
A bad example, which is
actually quite common,

00:24:34.380 --> 00:24:38.460
is alerting on CPU percentage
even though, again, autoscaling

00:24:38.460 --> 00:24:39.220
can handle it.

00:24:39.220 --> 00:24:45.100
So if autoscaling scales when
your CPUs are too stressed,

00:24:45.100 --> 00:24:47.160
you don't need to alert on that.

00:24:47.160 --> 00:24:50.760
Or another thing is
when issues go away.

00:24:50.760 --> 00:24:54.210
So you see this, it alerted,
it was red, now it's green,

00:24:54.210 --> 00:24:57.380
nobody looked at
it, and we go on.

00:24:57.380 --> 00:25:02.070
And it costs time, even if
nobody handled that alert.

00:25:02.070 --> 00:25:05.280
People look at it,
and it does cost time.

00:25:05.280 --> 00:25:06.600
And of course, necessary.

00:25:06.600 --> 00:25:10.980
So if it affects users, impacts
service, impacts business

00:25:10.980 --> 00:25:12.840
metrics.

00:25:12.840 --> 00:25:16.410
Anything else is not necessary.

00:25:16.410 --> 00:25:19.140
If it's-- or if it's covered
by other alerting rules,

00:25:19.140 --> 00:25:19.800
for example.

00:25:19.800 --> 00:25:23.940
So you see these alerts that
fire with other alerts together

00:25:23.940 --> 00:25:24.580
all the time.

00:25:24.580 --> 00:25:27.265
So great candidates for removal.

00:25:30.470 --> 00:25:34.490
Next, some best
practice examples

00:25:34.490 --> 00:25:36.440
of how to think about symptoms.

00:25:36.440 --> 00:25:38.930
So SLOs are a little
bit more obvious, right.

00:25:38.930 --> 00:25:43.790
You have uptime, latency,
errors, error ratios,

00:25:43.790 --> 00:25:46.670
but internal components,
you sometimes also

00:25:46.670 --> 00:25:48.530
have to alert on them, right?

00:25:48.530 --> 00:25:50.300
If you look at your
internal component

00:25:50.300 --> 00:25:51.850
and think of it as
a service that's

00:25:51.850 --> 00:25:54.830
servicing other
internal components,

00:25:54.830 --> 00:25:58.070
then you can figure out what
are the SLOs of that component

00:25:58.070 --> 00:26:02.060
towards its peers, and what are
kind of the black box metrics

00:26:02.060 --> 00:26:04.070
that you want to
look at when you're

00:26:04.070 --> 00:26:05.180
looking at that component.

00:26:05.180 --> 00:26:07.850
For example, if you take
batch jobs, for example.

00:26:07.850 --> 00:26:10.040
If they don't complete
on time, something

00:26:10.040 --> 00:26:14.090
is happening in there, but that
might affect other services

00:26:14.090 --> 00:26:17.250
and eventually that
will affect your users.

00:26:17.250 --> 00:26:20.297
So you do want to alert on
that, and that's actually--

00:26:20.297 --> 00:26:21.505
that is alerting on symptoms.

00:26:24.560 --> 00:26:27.260
THOMAS RAST: We do concede that
if you-- if you have your SLO

00:26:27.260 --> 00:26:29.720
alerts, sometimes there's
one more thing you really

00:26:29.720 --> 00:26:31.430
want to do, and we
call these cliffs.

00:26:31.430 --> 00:26:35.000
Because of how they measure
when the system fails

00:26:35.000 --> 00:26:37.750
catastrophically, as in
drives itself off of a cliff.

00:26:37.750 --> 00:26:40.220
And the typical case is
a resource alert, right.

00:26:40.220 --> 00:26:43.100
If you write your
user data to disk,

00:26:43.100 --> 00:26:46.460
and you run out of disks,
or disk quota, or whatever,

00:26:46.460 --> 00:26:48.610
you're not going to space today.

00:26:48.610 --> 00:26:52.700
And the advantage of then
having a 90% disk usage,

00:26:52.700 --> 00:26:55.970
say, for example, alert, which
is a very typical thing to do,

00:26:55.970 --> 00:26:57.420
is it gives you early warning.

00:26:57.420 --> 00:26:59.960
The SLO alerts would only
fire once you've actually

00:26:59.960 --> 00:27:04.130
exceeded your quota, and
things are actually broken.

00:27:04.130 --> 00:27:06.150
I want to call out
a distinction here.

00:27:06.150 --> 00:27:09.470
If SLO alerts are over paging
you and it's hurting you,

00:27:09.470 --> 00:27:11.690
you need to go and
revise the SLO.

00:27:11.690 --> 00:27:14.330
If a cliff alert is not giving
you good signal to noise,

00:27:14.330 --> 00:27:15.290
just delete it, right.

00:27:15.290 --> 00:27:17.540
There's nothing else to
adjust, just delete the alert.

00:27:21.360 --> 00:27:24.270
AMIR HERMELIN: So now we want
to spend the next few slides

00:27:24.270 --> 00:27:26.340
talking about the
human factor, which

00:27:26.340 --> 00:27:30.390
is very important in the
alerting equation, and really

00:27:30.390 --> 00:27:32.260
around two main things.

00:27:32.260 --> 00:27:38.640
One is what's called alerting
fatigue, or alerting frequency,

00:27:38.640 --> 00:27:40.740
and kind of alerting overload.

00:27:40.740 --> 00:27:43.830
And it's a big problem,
especially on services scale.

00:27:43.830 --> 00:27:46.140
And the other is
remembering that

00:27:46.140 --> 00:27:48.180
at the other end of
the alerting policy

00:27:48.180 --> 00:27:53.580
that you configure today,
another person, completely

00:27:53.580 --> 00:27:55.740
different person,
might be paged.

00:27:55.740 --> 00:27:57.100
So how do we deal with that?

00:27:57.100 --> 00:28:00.840
So firstly, with
frequency limits, Thomas,

00:28:00.840 --> 00:28:02.630
what have we learned
over the years?

00:28:02.630 --> 00:28:04.740
AMIR HERMELIN: So SRE has
a fairly firm guideline

00:28:04.740 --> 00:28:08.470
of having two outages per 12
hour shift on average, at most.

00:28:08.470 --> 00:28:10.590
You will note I said outages.

00:28:10.590 --> 00:28:13.590
If you have multiple pages
fighting for the same outage,

00:28:13.590 --> 00:28:15.600
it shouldn't be many,
right, but it's frequently

00:28:15.600 --> 00:28:19.810
hard to get it down to really
one on like, for every outage.

00:28:19.810 --> 00:28:21.390
So we don't insist on that.

00:28:21.390 --> 00:28:24.360
It should be a few, but the
two outages for 12 hour shifts

00:28:24.360 --> 00:28:25.620
are important.

00:28:25.620 --> 00:28:29.340
If you get significantly more
than this, you'll have burnout.

00:28:29.340 --> 00:28:32.310
This can be at an actual
individual level, where someone

00:28:32.310 --> 00:28:33.500
literally gets burn out.

00:28:33.500 --> 00:28:35.640
It can be at the team
level, where morale sinks,

00:28:35.640 --> 00:28:39.179
people leave, and you will get--

00:28:39.179 --> 00:28:41.220
sort of, I would call it
quality decrease, right?

00:28:41.220 --> 00:28:43.290
People don't consciously
ignore alerts,

00:28:43.290 --> 00:28:45.910
but subconsciously they kind of
no longer have the bandwidth,

00:28:45.910 --> 00:28:49.526
they do shallow investigations,
the postmortems don't really

00:28:49.526 --> 00:28:51.900
root cause anything, you're
never really fixing anything.

00:28:51.900 --> 00:28:55.254
And so you're never digging
yourself out of that hole.

00:28:55.254 --> 00:28:56.670
You might have far
fewer, though I

00:28:56.670 --> 00:29:00.270
have to say in a sort of second
law of thermodynamics sense,

00:29:00.270 --> 00:29:04.440
this never happens, because the
number of pages only increases,

00:29:04.440 --> 00:29:08.880
unless you have an active
investment to decrease it.

00:29:08.880 --> 00:29:10.620
But if this were to
happen, all right,

00:29:10.620 --> 00:29:13.390
you might have this,
consider two points.

00:29:13.390 --> 00:29:16.104
One is handling pages
gives you a lot of practice

00:29:16.104 --> 00:29:16.770
with the system.

00:29:16.770 --> 00:29:19.224
It's sort of the most
hands on thing you can do.

00:29:19.224 --> 00:29:21.390
You can compensate this,
you can do training, right,

00:29:21.390 --> 00:29:23.760
that's not a problem, but
you need to make an effort.

00:29:23.760 --> 00:29:26.250
And you may also ask
yourself is the investment

00:29:26.250 --> 00:29:28.771
in having SRE support
warranted if they're not even

00:29:28.771 --> 00:29:29.520
getting any pages.

00:29:32.390 --> 00:29:34.540
And I have some examples here.

00:29:34.540 --> 00:29:36.870
I want to go in some
detail into the first one,

00:29:36.870 --> 00:29:39.100
the alert cried wolf problem.

00:29:39.100 --> 00:29:41.350
We had a subsystem
of Monarch that

00:29:41.350 --> 00:29:45.680
would, on average, like once
or a couple of times per week,

00:29:45.680 --> 00:29:47.320
have a problem.

00:29:47.320 --> 00:29:49.770
And the problem would go
away, sometimes he would tweak

00:29:49.770 --> 00:29:51.490
a setting, sometimes
you wouldn't.

00:29:51.490 --> 00:29:54.489
Eventually it would
go away, and that

00:29:54.489 --> 00:29:56.780
that led to sort of this
conditioning that I mentioned.

00:29:56.780 --> 00:30:00.220
It's the subconscious you know
this isn't really a thing,

00:30:00.220 --> 00:30:02.650
and eventually, of course,
the inevitable happens

00:30:02.650 --> 00:30:04.280
and we had a real problem.

00:30:04.280 --> 00:30:05.987
And it was unnoticed for a week.

00:30:05.987 --> 00:30:07.570
Even though if you
knew where to look,

00:30:07.570 --> 00:30:09.736
you could tell that it was
a completely catastrophic

00:30:09.736 --> 00:30:10.290
failure.

00:30:10.290 --> 00:30:12.250
That system was not
making any progress.

00:30:12.250 --> 00:30:14.350
It was a [INAUDIBLE] pipeline.

00:30:14.350 --> 00:30:16.060
And of course,
like in proportion

00:30:16.060 --> 00:30:18.260
to it being stuck in
that state for a week,

00:30:18.260 --> 00:30:20.090
also that the magnitude
of the problem

00:30:20.090 --> 00:30:22.510
then increased, and you
really want to avoid this.

00:30:22.510 --> 00:30:24.280
Like, you see this
pattern when people

00:30:24.280 --> 00:30:25.870
say the alert went
away by itself,

00:30:25.870 --> 00:30:27.370
and then push back on it.

00:30:27.370 --> 00:30:29.620
The other two, I think, are
somewhat self-explanatory.

00:30:29.620 --> 00:30:31.078
I just wanted to
mention I observed

00:30:31.078 --> 00:30:33.340
all of these firsthand, all
right, and so all of this

00:30:33.340 --> 00:30:33.982
does happen.

00:30:33.982 --> 00:30:34.690
Watch out for it.

00:30:37.620 --> 00:30:42.140
AMIR HERMELIN: So this is how
fatigue actually looks like.

00:30:42.140 --> 00:30:44.450
This is a screenshot
from Stackdriver.

00:30:44.450 --> 00:30:49.340
The red lines and dots at the
top are alerting incidents,

00:30:49.340 --> 00:30:53.870
and this is a time span
from 5:00 AM to 8:00 AM.

00:30:53.870 --> 00:30:55.550
Now, you know, how
many of you want

00:30:55.550 --> 00:30:59.270
to be looking at
this at 8:00 AM?

00:30:59.270 --> 00:31:00.970
Raise your hands.

00:31:00.970 --> 00:31:03.380
Yeah, and you have
to go and figure out

00:31:03.380 --> 00:31:06.350
which of these alerts are real,
and how many issues you have,

00:31:06.350 --> 00:31:07.980
and what to do with them.

00:31:07.980 --> 00:31:09.800
You know, that's
clearly too many.

00:31:09.800 --> 00:31:13.070
At 8:00 AM, you know, you
want to be drinking coffee

00:31:13.070 --> 00:31:16.370
and reading the politics
section of the newspaper,

00:31:16.370 --> 00:31:18.110
but not looking at this.

00:31:18.110 --> 00:31:19.000
So that's an example.

00:31:21.800 --> 00:31:24.700
THOMAS RAST: When we're done,
on the first thing you do after

00:31:24.700 --> 00:31:28.180
getting a page, you will--
especially if it's an SLO based

00:31:28.180 --> 00:31:29.110
page, right--

00:31:29.110 --> 00:31:32.030
it's probably coming from a
prober, after what we told you.

00:31:32.030 --> 00:31:34.670
In a simple case, the prober
might have some backends.

00:31:34.670 --> 00:31:37.060
For simplicity, I put two.

00:31:37.060 --> 00:31:39.904
You'll want to know is the
problem with one of those,

00:31:39.904 --> 00:31:41.320
or is it somewhere
behind them, so

00:31:41.320 --> 00:31:42.790
that they uniformly get errors.

00:31:42.790 --> 00:31:44.590
And we call this drill down.

00:31:44.590 --> 00:31:47.770
And the drill down might look
something like these two graphs

00:31:47.770 --> 00:31:49.690
that we have on the next slide.

00:31:49.690 --> 00:31:50.980
And it tells you, right.

00:31:50.980 --> 00:31:53.470
In the first case, you probably
assume it's some back end,

00:31:53.470 --> 00:31:56.094
and the second case, clearly one
of the servers is responsible.

00:31:56.094 --> 00:31:59.500
You just need to hover over
the line and restart that guy.

00:31:59.500 --> 00:32:03.020
I mention this, because
frequently in a real example,

00:32:03.020 --> 00:32:05.350
you will have a load
balancer in between,

00:32:05.350 --> 00:32:07.090
and you need to think
about how you're

00:32:07.090 --> 00:32:11.440
going to do the signal
which back ends you picked.

00:32:11.440 --> 00:32:14.349
Like, how you're extracting
that from the prober

00:32:14.349 --> 00:32:15.640
before the pages happen, right?

00:32:15.640 --> 00:32:18.280
Because otherwise you're blind,
and you cannot drill down when

00:32:18.280 --> 00:32:19.988
you actually have
an SLO violation.

00:32:22.800 --> 00:32:25.310
AMIR HERMELIN: So
the other thing

00:32:25.310 --> 00:32:29.420
you want to do to help the
human is use dashboards

00:32:29.420 --> 00:32:31.520
and use what we
call documentation.

00:32:31.520 --> 00:32:36.390
In Stackdriver, sometimes it's
called referred to as Runbooks.

00:32:36.390 --> 00:32:41.150
You want to make sure that
whoever responds understands

00:32:41.150 --> 00:32:45.710
what the alert is about, what
was violated, what's the SLO.

00:32:45.710 --> 00:32:50.390
Understand the subsystem,
what subsystem it relates to,

00:32:50.390 --> 00:32:53.150
you know, in your service.

00:32:53.150 --> 00:32:55.970
Where to go, how to mitigate.

00:32:55.970 --> 00:32:59.150
You have to provide that,
especially up front,

00:32:59.150 --> 00:33:01.610
and the path to escalation.

00:33:01.610 --> 00:33:04.940
Now, the most common
mistakes that are made

00:33:04.940 --> 00:33:07.610
are around mitigation
and escalation,

00:33:07.610 --> 00:33:09.980
because when somebody
responds, especially

00:33:09.980 --> 00:33:13.340
if you're going meet
a high level of nines,

00:33:13.340 --> 00:33:15.175
the tendency is to
go and debug and fix.

00:33:15.175 --> 00:33:17.300
But actually, the first
thing you want to make sure

00:33:17.300 --> 00:33:20.150
is that the problem
is mitigated.

00:33:20.150 --> 00:33:21.860
Maybe it's already
mitigated, but it's

00:33:21.860 --> 00:33:23.810
going to crash
very soon, or maybe

00:33:23.810 --> 00:33:26.540
you have to do something about
it, like roll back, et cetera.

00:33:26.540 --> 00:33:28.590
That's the first
thing you should do.

00:33:28.590 --> 00:33:32.540
And then escalation,
whoever's in charge of this

00:33:32.540 --> 00:33:34.730
should provide clear
escalation path,

00:33:34.730 --> 00:33:40.070
and also, it's desirable to add
escalation timeline so people

00:33:40.070 --> 00:33:44.480
don't spend too much time
being stuck before they

00:33:44.480 --> 00:33:48.580
refer to their colleagues.

00:33:48.580 --> 00:33:50.890
So that was kind of--

00:33:50.890 --> 00:33:53.260
those last few slides were
about helping the human.

00:33:53.260 --> 00:33:58.330
Now we go into another thing,
which is notification methods.

00:33:58.330 --> 00:34:01.720
You want to have redundancy
with your notification methods,

00:34:01.720 --> 00:34:04.990
and not rely on a single
alerting notification methods.

00:34:04.990 --> 00:34:09.280
Because problems can occur at
the sending end, or problems

00:34:09.280 --> 00:34:12.520
can occur at the receiving
end, where the oncall is not

00:34:12.520 --> 00:34:15.610
able to receive that
type of notification.

00:34:15.610 --> 00:34:20.429
So if it's just n+1, on
the right side you have

00:34:20.429 --> 00:34:22.610
a Stackdriver screenshot.

00:34:22.610 --> 00:34:25.880
It's very easy to add additional
notification channels.

00:34:25.880 --> 00:34:28.540
The free tier includes email
and cloud console apps,

00:34:28.540 --> 00:34:31.270
so we recommend you
use both of them

00:34:31.270 --> 00:34:33.199
for all your alerting policies.

00:34:33.199 --> 00:34:36.310
If you have it, if you want
additional methods you can use

00:34:36.310 --> 00:34:39.340
oncall software like--

00:34:39.340 --> 00:34:41.920
and rotation software
like Pager Duty.

00:34:41.920 --> 00:34:48.489
You can use chat applications,
Webhook, or SMS to add to that.

00:34:48.489 --> 00:34:50.790
THOMAS RAST: I went to call
out SMS here as necessary.

00:34:50.790 --> 00:34:52.600
Not because it's
especially reliable,

00:34:52.600 --> 00:34:55.600
it totally isn't, but
it's the most independent

00:34:55.600 --> 00:34:57.490
from all the others among these.

00:34:57.490 --> 00:35:01.000
So if you pick three methods,
I suggest you put SMS there,

00:35:01.000 --> 00:35:03.100
because it gives
you an independence

00:35:03.100 --> 00:35:05.410
from all of the others
dying simultaneously

00:35:05.410 --> 00:35:07.690
to some issue in the system.

00:35:07.690 --> 00:35:10.150
And we have an example of
this actually happening.

00:35:10.150 --> 00:35:12.940
I mean, it's really embarrassing
if your pager, fails especially

00:35:12.940 --> 00:35:15.010
if you're on [INAUDIBLE]
and you're supposed

00:35:15.010 --> 00:35:17.240
to keep the pager running.

00:35:17.240 --> 00:35:19.371
We had an example, where
due to a mail loop, where

00:35:19.371 --> 00:35:21.370
every outgoing page would
loop back through mail

00:35:21.370 --> 00:35:24.040
and become a new page, we
delivered about 100 pages

00:35:24.040 --> 00:35:27.200
per second to a single person.

00:35:27.200 --> 00:35:29.418
Click and show the image.

00:35:29.418 --> 00:35:30.667
AMIR HERMELIN: Oh there he is.

00:35:30.667 --> 00:35:32.825
[LAUGHTER]

00:35:32.825 --> 00:35:35.450
THOMAS RAST: So I have to admit
this is for a different outage,

00:35:35.450 --> 00:35:37.590
but that one was
on a similar scale.

00:35:37.590 --> 00:35:40.490
The poor guy actually received
A large number of pages.

00:35:40.490 --> 00:35:42.120
What happened was, we--

00:35:42.120 --> 00:35:44.810
for the internal one, for
the internal paging app,

00:35:44.810 --> 00:35:46.990
we use an app engine,
Server Backend,

00:35:46.990 --> 00:35:49.310
and we just used up the
entire quota of that backend.

00:35:49.310 --> 00:35:51.470
And so it became
nonfunctional for all of SRE

00:35:51.470 --> 00:35:54.080
until someone managed to
intervene about 2 and 1/2 hours

00:35:54.080 --> 00:35:55.184
in or so.

00:35:55.184 --> 00:35:57.350
AMIR HERMELIN: I must say
he looks happy for someone

00:35:57.350 --> 00:35:58.310
that received so many--

00:35:58.310 --> 00:35:59.420
THOMAS RAST: He got a t-shirt.

00:35:59.420 --> 00:36:00.920
Engineers are always happy
when they get a t-shirt.

00:36:00.920 --> 00:36:02.930
AMIR HERMELIN: That's
totally worth it.

00:36:02.930 --> 00:36:05.030
Something that you might
be more familiar with,

00:36:05.030 --> 00:36:07.180
because it was
very, very public,

00:36:07.180 --> 00:36:11.780
is the DNS outage of late 2016.

00:36:11.780 --> 00:36:15.470
Many, many prominent services
went down, and you know,

00:36:15.470 --> 00:36:19.130
even though our services
were still up and running,

00:36:19.130 --> 00:36:21.620
a lot of third parties
were experiencing failure.

00:36:21.620 --> 00:36:23.480
So we got a lot
of support calls,

00:36:23.480 --> 00:36:25.430
because users were relying
on one service that

00:36:25.430 --> 00:36:28.820
might have gone down, or
the APIs are not available,

00:36:28.820 --> 00:36:31.970
or the email gets rate
limited, so again, very

00:36:31.970 --> 00:36:35.940
important to have
redundancy with the way

00:36:35.940 --> 00:36:40.460
your alerts are
getting delivered.

00:36:40.460 --> 00:36:45.800
So here are some additional best
practices, kind of each one,

00:36:45.800 --> 00:36:48.350
each slide stands on its own.

00:36:48.350 --> 00:36:49.500
One thing is grouping.

00:36:49.500 --> 00:36:51.920
So you want to
reduce your alerts,

00:36:51.920 --> 00:36:56.150
especially similar
alerts that kind of--

00:36:56.150 --> 00:36:58.010
policies that do the
same thing for a bunch

00:36:58.010 --> 00:37:00.650
of resources that act in unison.

00:37:00.650 --> 00:37:03.440
You want to group them
together into a single policy.

00:37:03.440 --> 00:37:06.020
If its Stackdriver, you
can use Stackdriver groups,

00:37:06.020 --> 00:37:11.360
which is kind of a concept that
is inherent in the platform.

00:37:11.360 --> 00:37:14.260
But think about
when problems occur,

00:37:14.260 --> 00:37:17.270
you don't want to
get paged 100 times.

00:37:17.270 --> 00:37:19.340
You want one alerting
policy to fire,

00:37:19.340 --> 00:37:21.650
and then you know
that it relates

00:37:21.650 --> 00:37:24.540
to a group of resources.

00:37:24.540 --> 00:37:26.180
One thing to note,
though, and remember

00:37:26.180 --> 00:37:28.160
is that if you're
doing this, you

00:37:28.160 --> 00:37:30.800
have to have efficient
drill down mechanisms,

00:37:30.800 --> 00:37:33.830
because in many cases,
when that alert fires,

00:37:33.830 --> 00:37:36.470
you will have to kind
of investigate and see

00:37:36.470 --> 00:37:39.230
which of the resources, which
components of the group,

00:37:39.230 --> 00:37:41.900
are misbehaving.

00:37:41.900 --> 00:37:46.160
And you know, that 100 page
storm that we saw earlier

00:37:46.160 --> 00:37:48.620
might sound funny,
and that person

00:37:48.620 --> 00:37:51.290
was smiling after
he got the t-shirt,

00:37:51.290 --> 00:37:55.390
but it does happen every year
or two, even inside of Google.

00:37:55.390 --> 00:37:58.927
So be prepared for that,
and be ahead of the curve.

00:37:58.927 --> 00:38:01.010
THOMAS RAST: You know how
to [INAUDIBLE] 100 pages

00:38:01.010 --> 00:38:02.507
that arrive simultaneously?

00:38:02.507 --> 00:38:06.800
AMIR HERMELIN: You do a lot of--

00:38:06.800 --> 00:38:09.560
THOMAS RAST: So we have two more
slides on process, just like,

00:38:09.560 --> 00:38:10.970
some brief notes.

00:38:10.970 --> 00:38:13.200
If you organize a
non-code rotation,

00:38:13.200 --> 00:38:15.029
our guideline in SRE is for--

00:38:15.029 --> 00:38:16.820
I mean, that's for a
quick response, right.

00:38:16.820 --> 00:38:18.800
24/7 coverage, five
minute response time

00:38:18.800 --> 00:38:21.740
to working on the problem,
being at the laptop.

00:38:21.740 --> 00:38:24.470
You want 2 times 6 people
in different time zones,

00:38:24.470 --> 00:38:26.917
or eight people in
the same time zone.

00:38:26.917 --> 00:38:28.500
Again, this is just
a burnout problem.

00:38:28.500 --> 00:38:30.749
They'll be on call too much,
if you have fewer people,

00:38:30.749 --> 00:38:32.820
and then they'll
burn out over time.

00:38:32.820 --> 00:38:35.420
Similar to how you have
n+1 notification methods,

00:38:35.420 --> 00:38:38.074
you should probably have
n+1 people who get notified.

00:38:38.074 --> 00:38:40.490
I don't know, the primary might
be on the New York subway,

00:38:40.490 --> 00:38:42.230
they don't have any
reception there.

00:38:42.230 --> 00:38:43.944
So we recommend you
have a secondary.

00:38:43.944 --> 00:38:45.860
This could be someone
from some other rotation

00:38:45.860 --> 00:38:49.189
or someone on the team, and
then if that still doesn't work,

00:38:49.189 --> 00:38:51.230
you escalate to the entire
team, because you just

00:38:51.230 --> 00:38:54.520
need a panic mode, I guess.

00:38:54.520 --> 00:38:57.305
And what's really worked
for us is having--

00:38:57.305 --> 00:38:59.180
I mean, if you're doing
weekly shift anyways,

00:38:59.180 --> 00:39:00.560
it makes a lot of
sense, having a weekly

00:39:00.560 --> 00:39:01.730
on call handoff meeting.

00:39:01.730 --> 00:39:03.771
This should only take, I
don't now, half an hour.

00:39:06.200 --> 00:39:10.520
And postmortem process.

00:39:10.520 --> 00:39:14.170
I mean, you probably know
the basic drill, right?

00:39:14.170 --> 00:39:16.670
One thing to call out here is
we generally write postmortems

00:39:16.670 --> 00:39:19.020
when either the outage
was announced widely,

00:39:19.020 --> 00:39:22.007
even internally, or
there was user impact.

00:39:22.007 --> 00:39:24.590
But this is somewhat circular,
because in the other direction,

00:39:24.590 --> 00:39:26.782
the saying goes, you should
really announce widely,

00:39:26.782 --> 00:39:28.490
internally, at least,
if you think you're

00:39:28.490 --> 00:39:30.780
going to write a postmortem.

00:39:30.780 --> 00:39:34.420
People learn and develop
some experience for this.

00:39:34.420 --> 00:39:37.050
And one thing to remember
in the context of alerting,

00:39:37.050 --> 00:39:39.210
is it's somewhat
tempting in a postmortem

00:39:39.210 --> 00:39:42.960
to draw as an action
item, add an alert.

00:39:42.960 --> 00:39:45.970
This should not usually be
your only action item, right.

00:39:45.970 --> 00:39:48.900
It's justified to
add an alert, if you

00:39:48.900 --> 00:39:52.450
could have detected the problem
sooner by having this alert,

00:39:52.450 --> 00:39:54.750
but you should still go
and fix the root cause.

00:39:54.750 --> 00:39:57.000
And postmortems that say add
an alert and nothing else

00:39:57.000 --> 00:39:59.583
are how you get into a situation
where you have page overload.

00:40:02.400 --> 00:40:04.950
AMIR HERMELIN: So with
all these best practices,

00:40:04.950 --> 00:40:08.370
we didn't say anything
about Google platform yet,

00:40:08.370 --> 00:40:10.800
and we didn't include any plug.

00:40:10.800 --> 00:40:18.550
So the best best practice,
in my mind, is that running--

00:40:18.550 --> 00:40:22.120
remember, running monitoring
yourself is a lot of overhead,

00:40:22.120 --> 00:40:25.720
and folks like Thomas
are hard to come by

00:40:25.720 --> 00:40:28.850
and they're not free.

00:40:28.850 --> 00:40:32.620
So the best thing
to do is actually

00:40:32.620 --> 00:40:34.834
let somebody else
do your monitoring.

00:40:34.834 --> 00:40:37.000
Additionally, if you're
running your own monitoring,

00:40:37.000 --> 00:40:40.300
you have to make sure that
it's running correctly,

00:40:40.300 --> 00:40:44.080
which is called metamonitoring,
monitoring your monitoring.

00:40:44.080 --> 00:40:48.490
Instead, Google Cloud Platform
does offer a Stackdriver,

00:40:48.490 --> 00:40:50.740
and as I mentioned it's
more than just monitoring.

00:40:50.740 --> 00:40:52.950
It's an integrated
suite of products

00:40:52.950 --> 00:40:55.690
that includes
monitoring, logging,

00:40:55.690 --> 00:41:00.070
alerting functionality, trace
debug and error reporting,

00:41:00.070 --> 00:41:01.960
and it's very, very
tightly integrated.

00:41:01.960 --> 00:41:05.740
So for example, a log that you
ingest goes into the logging

00:41:05.740 --> 00:41:08.590
and you can analyze it there,
but if that's an error,

00:41:08.590 --> 00:41:10.720
it can also trigger
error reporting.

00:41:10.720 --> 00:41:12.400
It could also trigger an alert.

00:41:12.400 --> 00:41:15.280
You can take that log and
extract metrics from it,

00:41:15.280 --> 00:41:19.430
put it in dashboards, alert on
these, or do whatever you want.

00:41:19.430 --> 00:41:22.030
So the nice thing about
it is it's all integrated,

00:41:22.030 --> 00:41:24.280
out of the box,
great experience.

00:41:24.280 --> 00:41:28.900
And there's a free trial,
there's also a free tier,

00:41:28.900 --> 00:41:31.390
you know, if you're not
using it to a large extent,

00:41:31.390 --> 00:41:32.950
you could stay on the free tier.

00:41:32.950 --> 00:41:36.310
But even premium's pricing
is very, very competitive.

00:41:36.310 --> 00:41:38.050
To our discussion
today, it's also

00:41:38.050 --> 00:41:41.680
geared towards helping you
utilize your time better,

00:41:41.680 --> 00:41:44.830
and actually implement best
practices around alerting.

00:41:44.830 --> 00:41:46.660
So it does offer
uptime checks to help

00:41:46.660 --> 00:41:51.865
you focus around SLOs, to help
you focus around symptoms.

00:41:51.865 --> 00:41:56.320
It offers multiple notification
methods, and Runbooks,

00:41:56.320 --> 00:41:58.990
and other kind of
custom dashboards

00:41:58.990 --> 00:42:02.950
to help the person that's
responding respond efficiently

00:42:02.950 --> 00:42:09.100
to alert notifications.

00:42:09.100 --> 00:42:12.590
It offers great
drill down component,

00:42:12.590 --> 00:42:17.950
so really, we take
our experience here

00:42:17.950 --> 00:42:20.535
and we use it when we're
implementing these services.

00:42:23.100 --> 00:42:27.260
So let's see if we
remember everything.

00:42:27.260 --> 00:42:30.350
If you have to remember
just one slide,

00:42:30.350 --> 00:42:32.300
remember the one with
the guy in the t-shirt.

00:42:32.300 --> 00:42:35.480
No, just kidding,
remember this slide.

00:42:35.480 --> 00:42:36.780
Let's quickly go over it.

00:42:36.780 --> 00:42:39.500
The four golden signals, you
should be monitoring them.

00:42:39.500 --> 00:42:42.200
Latency and traffic,
how your service is

00:42:42.200 --> 00:42:45.710
viewed from the outside, and--

00:42:45.710 --> 00:42:47.580
sorry latency and
errors, how your service

00:42:47.580 --> 00:42:50.450
is viewed from the outside,
traffic and saturation, how

00:42:50.450 --> 00:42:56.080
your service looks from
the outside inside.

00:42:56.080 --> 00:42:58.400
JUAN, every alert
policy should be

00:42:58.400 --> 00:43:02.660
tested to meet these criteria.

00:43:02.660 --> 00:43:05.540
Judgment, every alert
should be Urgent,

00:43:05.540 --> 00:43:09.300
should be Actionable,
and definitely Necessary.

00:43:09.300 --> 00:43:14.650
You should be alerting mostly if
not entirely only on symptoms,

00:43:14.650 --> 00:43:17.720
and these include
SLOs, and you can also

00:43:17.720 --> 00:43:22.820
alert on cliffs that
will induce symptoms.

00:43:22.820 --> 00:43:27.350
Don't forget the human factor,
so limit alert fatigue.

00:43:27.350 --> 00:43:32.030
Limit the amount of pages that
somebody can get in a shift.

00:43:32.030 --> 00:43:35.960
Built in debugability,
building the ability

00:43:35.960 --> 00:43:39.230
to mitigate, and help
the oncaller mitigate,

00:43:39.230 --> 00:43:44.720
and provide a clear escalation
path for when people get stuck.

00:43:44.720 --> 00:43:48.440
More than one notification
method is the next thing.

00:43:48.440 --> 00:43:52.040
Use email, use the cloud
console app, SMS, whatever,

00:43:52.040 --> 00:43:54.590
but use more than one.

00:43:54.590 --> 00:43:57.890
And lastly, remember that the
process is iterative, right.

00:43:57.890 --> 00:44:02.105
So you have to
respond efficiently,

00:44:02.105 --> 00:44:04.740
you have to configure
your alerts efficiently,

00:44:04.740 --> 00:44:07.640
you have to mitigate,
and also postmortem.

00:44:07.640 --> 00:44:09.140
Even when the problem
is gone, let's

00:44:09.140 --> 00:44:12.710
make things efficient
the next time around.

00:44:12.710 --> 00:44:16.660
[MUSIC PLAYING]

